abstract: "263 published species-level phylogenies redistributed as Newick files with numeric tip ids: the largest species-level tree found for each of 24 groups of described eukaryotes and for Bacteria and Archaea, and 237 other trees. A name layer resolves every tip id to a standardized name and every reference-tree tip to a Catalogue of Life (COL26.9) accepted name. Registers record, for each reference tree, its molecular tips, whether it is a chronogram in absolute time, and the divergence-time interval its source archive carries, from which the coverage of the 2,159,542 described eukaryotic species is counted at four levels: named, placed with molecular data, dated, and dated with an interval that a calibration does not set. scripts/reproduce_paper.py recomputes every number of the accompanying paper from these files. The source deposits remain in their original archives; the TimeTree of Life is cited and not redistributed."
